On Apr 16, 7:32 am, Rob Ellwood <rob.ellw...@shaw.ca.invalid> wrote:
> Don't let barbarians gain skills in any spell class, not even
> combat spells.  Take away their special spell.

This wouldn't really be that much of a change.  The barbarian's
innate spell-casting penalty is so high, they typically get 100%
fail rates even on low-level spells anyhow, until well into the
middle game if not Gehennom.  Gaining skill in the attack spell
class would require *successfully* casting attack spells, but by
the time a barbarian can cast force bolt at a decent success rate
their physical stats and weapon skills are so advanced that they
can do MUCH more damage per turn in melee anyway.  Also, chaotic
barbarians typically need a cloak of magic resistance (and thus no
robe) or a shield of reflection, if not both, so casting spells in
combat is effectively a non-option.  (Neutral barbarians can get an
MR artifact and SDSM, admittedly.)

As for "haste self", the fact that it's "special" for barbarians is
barely relevant.  As a level three spell, the barbarian is unlikely
to be able to cast it until pretty late in the game, special status
notwithstanding.  By that time, barbarians are well past XL7 and
have a decent amount of intrinsic speed for free.  For that matter,
most barbarians will have the castle wand (and thus speed boots if
desired) before they can even *read* a level-three spellbook.

I'm not saying these changes would be *bad* (I'm generally in favor
of increasing the differentiation between the roles), but I'm not
at all sure they're necessary.


